## Kev request concurrency checkpoint (2026-10-12)

- Current self-hosted Kev profile selected with `model: laya/kev` handles
  inference sequentially: its per-profile IPC lock serializes records, and one
  Kev child process handles requests in sequence. OmniRoute accepts concurrent
  HTTP requests, which queue at Kev. ONNX CPU threads do not mean separate
  inference jobs run concurrently.
- Two ~1k-chunk requests produced the same successful outputs and routes in
  both runs. Sequential pair: 13.466s total (7.094s, 6.371s). Concurrent HTTP
  pair: 12.449s total (first 6.417s, second 12.447s), showing queueing rather
  than concurrent Kev inference. Caller concurrency should be one for this
  profile. This is not evidence about other Laya profiles or other OmniRoute
  upstreams.
- Deployment caps `/opt/kev-model/kev.json` at 8192 for both
  `max_state_tokens` and `max_row_tokens`. A prior oversized request returned
  502 to its caller at 180s while Kev continued processing; HTTP timeout does
  not cancel inference. Worker-pool concurrency remains future work, not
  current behavior.
- Documentation checks rerun with Node 24: docs sync, frontmatter, counts,
  links, and fabricated-doc checks pass. `check:docs-all` stops at existing
  environment sync drift: `GITHUB_API_URL`, `GITHUB_REF`, and
  `GITHUB_REPOSITORY` are referenced by code but absent from `.env.example`;
  none were added by this change. Counts report only three soft warnings for
  unrelated executor/OAuth docs. Deprecated-version scan reports 97 existing
  candidate findings. Markdownlint reports 706 issues in three source docs;
  unchanged HEAD copies report the same 706, so this change adds none.

Date: 2026-10-03
Scope: reviewed local implementation of `add-auto-band-seam`,
`add-auto-quality-band-filter`, `add-auto-band-demand-reserve`, and
`add-systemone-decisions-route`. Calibration implementation and local review
are complete. Production deployment and migration are complete; real-catalog
validation, authenticated route checks, remaining live-service checks, and
archiving the four remaining OpenSpec packages remain open. The production
webpack memory and official-image overlay packages have been archived and
their deltas merged into living specs.

## Decisions and implemented behavior

- Band channels default to `thrifty` only while `OMNIROUTE_AUTO_BANDS` is on.
  With the flag off, an omitted tier stays unset for upstream handling and an
  explicit tier keeps upstream behavior.
- Unrated models pass only `low`; they receive no synthetic score and stay out
  of `mid` and `high`. The old `agt` role maps to `general + tools`.
- Enabled band `thrifty` channels order eligible rungs `free → keyless →
subscription → cheap → premium`. Plain upstream `auto:thrifty` behavior is
  unchanged. Explicit `:free` retains upstream provider/model classification,
  including eligible keyless OpenCode models; it is not a per-connection
  zero-spend guarantee.
- Calibration cut points use sorted distinct numeric scores from observed
  rated models. The user approved nearest-rank selection:
  `c1 = scores[ceil(0.33*N)-1]`, `c2 = scores[ceil(0.67*N)-1]`; for nine
  distinct values, select the third and seventh. The rule is recorded in the
  quality design/spec. The calibration minimum-population guard counts
  candidates retained by each band, including unrated candidates admitted to
  `low`.
- The user approved maximum known positive monthly capacity per
  `(account, provider, poolKey)`. The disabled reserve state is a full stop:
  no source reads, calculations, refresh, diagnostics, or account narrowing.
- The user explicitly authorized deployment, production migration, and
  activation. The supported Node 26 candidate build/start, fresh-copy
  migration checks, and production cutover have passed. Radar is enabled and
  public opt-in is true; its sync request returned HTTP 200, but the follow-up
  status reports `error`, so feed/cache activation is not yet verified.
  Authenticated route checks and remaining live readiness checks are pending.

## Implementation and review

Band grammar, quality/capability filters, the selector and fallback ordering,
exact provider/model/account mappings, quota reservation, account allowlist
narrowing, reserve configuration, demand/capacity calculations, and reserve
refresh diagnostics are implemented. Disabled paths preserve upstream routing.
The account selector retains account B when a stale direct pin names A but the
authoritative allowlist is `[B]`; quota-blocked accounts remain after routable
rungs and preserve the existing final 429 when all candidates are blocked.

The confirmed quality-selection issue is fixed: strategies now run within each
billing rung, rather than allowing a global premium score or LKGP pin to outrank
eligible free candidates. The quota-cutoff regression also passes. Independent
review closed both routing findings, the quality hook, reserve configuration,
capacity behavior, and reserve integration. The identical-positive daily-cap
regression now retains one known daily axis in either catalog order; differing
positive caps still fail refresh and preserve the prior snapshot.

Three independently confirmed SystemOne defects are fixed and closed:

- Bounded BYOB reads copy small chunks without retaining near-1 MiB backing
  buffers.
- A late upstream success cannot clear a newer concurrent failure cooldown.
- Upstream error text is not persisted into call-log summaries; client error
  responses keep their specified shape.

The earlier config recovery finding is also fixed and independently verified:
7/7 focused tests cover same-mtime `EACCES` recovery and malformed-range
warning deduplication. SystemOne routing/auth alias checks found no auth seam.

Five independently confirmed calibration findings are fixed and closed:

- Manual mode now bounds/clears observed model IDs without waiting for a
  calibration run.
- The `minPerBand` guard counts unrated candidates admitted to `low`.
- Guarded-attempt timing persists so a restart does not rerun calibration
  before `intervalHours`.
- The CLI report waits for cold config loading before reading configured
  `ratedSources` and structured-output policy.
- The first request after restart uses saved ranges before constructing the
  enabled predicate, avoiding a default-range first request.

The approved formula uses nearest-rank cut points from sorted distinct numeric
scores. The focused CLI smoke used an isolated DB and returned valid JSON for 10
baseline models, 0 rated models, and no cut points; it is not a real-catalog
report.

## Verification

- Auto-Combo suite before calibration: 40 files / 353 tests passed; core and OpenSSE typechecks
  passed. Scoped checks: quality E2E 4/4, reserve source tests 25/25 across
  three suites, reserve account-routing E2E 4/4, config 13/13, capabilities
  8/8, filter 8/8, billing-rung routing 7/7, and direct upstream
  resolver/stream/quota tests 27/27. Independent review closed these slices.
- Calibration tests pass 19/19; the combined Auto-Combo and service regression
  passes 41 files / 372 tests with two workers. Core/OpenSSE typechecks,
